#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a push whose commits are not gofmt-clean, the check CI starts with.
#
# # What this is for
#
# The Go job in .github/workflows/ci.yml opens with `gofmt -l .` and fails on
# any output. A failing first step skips every step after it: vet, the -race
# tests, the docs server build, the WASM build and wasm/verify. From 987e755
# to 7655b91 that happened on seven master pushes in a row, and nobody noticed
# because the Android, iOS and docs jobs are separate workflows' jobs and stayed
# green. The cause was one missing blank line in core/style_props.go. So a red
# gofmt step does more than mark a style problem. It stops the tests from
# running on master at all.
#
# This runs the same command locally, before the push leaves the machine.
#
# # Why it blocks, where .claude/hooks/session-doc-check.sh only warns
#
# That hook warns because it often misjudges: a typo fix or a rebase owes no
# session doc. This check can't be wrong that way, because it is the same
# command CI runs, on the same files. A push it refuses is a push CI would fail.
# `git push --no-verify` still skips it for the rare push that is meant to be
# red.
#
# # What it checks: the pushed commits, not the working tree
#
# `gofmt -l .` in the checkout would look at the wrong files in two directions:
#
#   - Uncommitted edits. A file fixed on disk but not committed passes here
#     and still fails CI. A half-edited file that is not being pushed fails
#     here and blocks an unrelated push.
#   - Untracked files. CI's checkout has only tracked files. A local checkout
#     also has .claude/worktrees/ (whole checkouts of other branches, ignored
#     through .git/info/exclude) and any scratch file, and gofmt walks
#     directories without reading ignore rules.
#
# So each pushed commit's tracked Go files are exported with `git archive`
# into a temporary directory, and `gofmt -l .` runs there. That tree matches
# what actions/checkout gives CI, minus the non-Go files, which gofmt skips
# anyway. The export and the check take about 0.2s for this repository's ~500
# Go files.
#
#   stdin (one line per ref)          per distinct commit
#   ─────────────────────────         ──────────────────────────────────────
#   <local ref> <local sha>    ──►    git archive <sha> -- '*.go' | tar -x
#   <remote ref> <remote sha>         cd tmp && gofmt -l .   ──► any output?
#                                                                 │
#                                              yes: list, exit 1 ◄┘ no: next
#
# # Every commit, by default
#
# Each ref's new commits are checked, oldest first, not only its tip. CI
# checks the tip alone, so an intermediate commit that a later commit in the
# same push fixes never turns CI red. It still leaves a history that is not
# clean commit by commit, and that costs other things: `git bisect run go vet
# ./...` stops at a commit that does not build, and a cherry-pick onto a
# release branch carries the one commit, not the fix that followed it.
#
#   remote already has <remote sha>?  ── yes ──► git rev-list <remote>..<local>
#       │ no (a new branch, or a force push over commits this clone lacks)
#       ▼
#   git rev-list <local> --not --remotes   (the commits no remote-tracking
#                                            ref has yet)
#
# This was opt-in while a commit was reported for every unformatted file in
# its tree, including files it never touched: one bad commit early in a push
# was reported again under every commit after it, and a file that
# already-pushed history left unformatted blocked every commit of every push
# until someone fixed it. The attribution below removed both, so the stricter
# check became the default. For the tip only, as CI checks:
#
#     git config grmob.prepush.everyCommit false
#
# (or `git -c grmob.prepush.everyCommit=false push` for one push).
#
# # Which commit a file is reported against
#
# Each commit's whole Go tree is still exported and checked, the same as a
# tip, so what is measured stays CI's own command. Only the report is sorted,
# by `git diff --name-only <first parent> <commit>`, and each file's origin is
# carried from commit to commit in a small status file per commit:
#
#   an unformatted file in a commit      reported as               blocks
#   ───────────────────────────────      ───────────────────────   ──────
#   changed by this commit (or a root    listed under the commit   yes
#     commit, where every file is new)
#   unchanged since a parent that        a count, "listed above"   yes
#     introduced it in this push
#   unchanged since before this push     listed once, then a       only at the
#                                          count, "noted above"    ref's tip
#
# The last row is why the tip is special. A file that already-pushed history
# left unformatted is no fault of the commits that carry it, and commits
# already on a remote cannot be rewritten to fix it, so blocking every commit
# on it would only teach --no-verify. The tip still blocks on it, because the
# tip is what CI's gofmt runs on, and CI would fail.
#
# A merge is compared with its first parent, so a file a merge brings in from
# its second parent counts as the merge's change, the answer
# `git log --first-parent` gives too. `--no-renames` lists a renamed file
# under its new name, which is the name gofmt reports. When the diff cannot be
# taken, every file counts as the commit's own: the report is noisier, and the
# push outcome is the stricter one.
#
# More than EVERY_COMMIT_MAX commits in one ref (a first push of a long branch
# to a fresh remote, where --not --remotes excludes nothing) checks the tip
# only and says so: at about 0.2s a commit, a few hundred would hold the push
# for a minute to answer a question the tip already mostly answers.
#
# # Enabling it
#
# Git runs hooks from .git/hooks/, which is not tracked, so a clone does not
# pick this file up by itself. Point this checkout at the tracked directory
# once:
#
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# core.hooksPath is per clone, so every clone needs this line once.
#
# # Failure modes that let the push through
#
# If no gofmt can be found, or a tip cannot be exported (for example, it has no Go
# files, or it is a tag that does not point at a tree), this prints why and
# skips it. Such a push never runs the Go job's gofmt step on a Go tree, so
# there is nothing here to stop, and a hook that errors on setups it cannot
# handle is a hook that gets deleted.

set -u

# The all-zero object name git sends for a ref being deleted. Its length
# follows the repository's hash (40 hex digits for SHA-1, 64 for SHA-256), so
# the check below matches any run of zeros rather than a fixed-length string.
is_zero_sha() {
    case "$1" in
    *[!0]*) return 1 ;;
    *) return 0 ;;
    esac
}

# Which gofmt: the one go.mod selects, not whichever is first on PATH.
#
# CI installs Go with `go-version-file: go.mod`, so its gofmt is that release's.
# A machine's PATH gofmt can be older or newer, and gofmt's output does change
# between releases now and then (1.19 reformatted doc comments), which would
# make this hook and CI disagree in either direction.
#
# `go env GOROOT`, run from the repository root, answers with the toolchain the
# go command selects for this module. Under GOTOOLCHAIN=auto that is go.mod's
# `go`/`toolchain` line, downloaded into the module cache if the installed Go
# is older. Git runs a pre-push hook from the top of the work tree, so the
# go.mod read is this repository's.
#
#   go on PATH?  ── no ──► gofmt on PATH? ── no ──► skip, with a message
#       │ yes                  │ yes
#       ▼                      ▼
#   $(go env GOROOT)/bin/gofmt exists? ── no ──► PATH gofmt, with a note
#       │ yes
#       ▼
#   that gofmt
#
# A failed `go env` (an offline machine that needs a toolchain download, say)
# falls back to PATH's gofmt and says so, rather than skipping: an approximate
# check is still worth more here than none.
gofmt_bin=""
if command -v go >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    goroot=$(go env GOROOT 2>/dev/null) || goroot=""
    if [ -n "$goroot" ] && [ -x "$goroot/bin/gofmt" ]; then
        gofmt_bin="$goroot/bin/gofmt"
    fi
fi
if [ -z "$gofmt_bin" ]; then
    if ! command -v gofmt >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "pre-push: gofmt not on PATH; skipping the gofmt check." >&2
        exit 0
    fi
    gofmt_bin=$(command -v gofmt)
    if command -v go >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "pre-push: could not locate go.mod's toolchain; using $gofmt_bin, which may format differently from CI." >&2
    fi
fi

tmp_root=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/grmob-prepush.XXXXXX") || {
    echo "pre-push: could not create a temporary directory; skipping the gofmt check." >&2
    exit 0
}
trap 'rm -rf "$tmp_root"' EXIT INT TERM

# Read all of stdin before doing any work. Git writes the ref list into a pipe,
# and a hook that exits early without draining it can make git report a
# broken pipe instead of the hook's own message.
refs=$(cat)

checked=""
failed=0

# See "Every commit, by default". `--bool` normalises no/off/0 to "false", and
# only that value turns the per-commit check off. An unset key makes `git
# config` exit 1 and an unparseable value makes it fail; both fall to the
# default, so a typo in the setting never quietly loosens the check.
every=$(git config --bool --get grmob.prepush.everyCommit 2>/dev/null) || every="true"
EVERY_COMMIT_MAX=100

# check_tree <label> <object> [<mode>]: export the object's tracked Go files,
# run gofmt over them, and report what it finds. A function so the tip and
# each commit of an every-commit push go through one path. It runs in the
# calling shell, not a subshell, so the assignment to failed is seen by the
# caller.
#
#   mode     used for                          an unformatted file
#   ────     ────────                          ───────────────────
#   tip      the tip-only check (the default   listed; blocks
#            argument)
#   commit   a commit before a ref's tip       sorted by attribute; blocks
#                                              unless it predates the push
#   last     the tip, in an every-commit run   sorted by attribute; blocks
check_tree() {
    label=$1
    object=$2
    mode=${3:-tip}

    # A branch and a tag pushed together often name the same commit (a release
    # push), and in every-commit mode two refs share their older commits.
    # Check each object once.
    case " $checked " in
    *" $object "*) return 0 ;;
    esac
    checked="$checked $object"

    tree_dir="$tmp_root/$object"
    mkdir -p "$tree_dir"

    # The archive goes to a file, not straight into `tar -x` through a pipe.
    # A pipeline's status is its last command's, and tar given empty input
    # exits 0, so `git archive … | tar -x` would read a failed export as a tree
    # with nothing unformatted in it: a pass that checked nothing.
    #
    # `git archive` exits 128 both when the pathspec matches nothing (a tip
    # with no Go files) and when the object is not a tree-ish. Either way there
    # is no Go tree for CI's gofmt step to fail on, so git's own reason is
    # printed and the object is skipped.
    archive="$tmp_root/$object.tar"
    if ! git archive -o "$archive" "$object" -- '*.go' 2>"$tmp_root/archive.err"; then
        echo "pre-push: nothing to check for $label: $(cat "$tmp_root/archive.err")" >&2
        return 0
    fi
    if ! tar -xf "$archive" -C "$tree_dir"; then
        echo "pre-push: could not unpack $label; skipping it." >&2
        return 0
    fi

    # `gofmt -l` exits 0 whether or not it lists anything, the same thing the
    # CI step's comment notes, so an unformatted file is detected from the
    # output. A file gofmt cannot parse is different: gofmt prints the error
    # to stderr and exits 2, and GitHub runs `run:` blocks under `bash -e`,
    # where `unformatted=$(gofmt -l .)` takes that status and fails the step.
    # stderr is captured separately so that case also blocks here.
    unformatted=$(cd "$tree_dir" && "$gofmt_bin" -l . 2>"$tmp_root/gofmt.err")
    if [ -s "$tmp_root/gofmt.err" ]; then
        echo "pre-push: gofmt reported errors for $label:" >&2
        sed 's/^/    /' "$tmp_root/gofmt.err" >&2
        failed=1
    fi
    [ -n "$unformatted" ] || return 0
    files=$(printf '%s\n' "$unformatted" | sed 's|^\./||')
    if [ "$mode" = "tip" ]; then
        echo "pre-push: $label is not gofmt-clean:" >&2
        printf '%s\n' "$files" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
        failed=1
        return 0
    fi
    attribute "$label" "$object" "$mode" "$files"
    return 0
}

# attribute <label> <commit> <mode> <files>: report a commit's unformatted
# files by where each came from, and set failed=1 when one of them blocks. See
# "Which commit a file is reported against" for the table this implements.
#
# Each file's origin is written to $tmp_root/<commit>.status as "pushed
# <path>" (introduced by a commit of this push) or "before <path>" (carried in
# from history the push did not add), so the next commit can inherit it
# instead of being blamed. Commits are checked oldest first, so a parent's
# status file exists whenever the parent was checked in this run and had
# anything to report; with no status file the parent was not part of the
# push, and an unchanged file is from before it.
#
#   file in <files>
#     │
#     ├─ no parent, or changed since it ──► own     "pushed"   listed, blocks
#     ├─ parent says "pushed <path>"    ──► count   "pushed"   blocks
#     ├─ parent says "before <path>"    ──► count   "before"   blocks at last
#     └─ parent has no status           ──► listed  "before"   blocks at last
#
# The while loop reads a here-document rather than a pipe, so it runs in this
# shell and its counters survive the loop.
attribute() {
    label=$1
    object=$2
    mode=$3
    files=$4

    # A root commit has no first parent; `--verify -q` answers empty for it
    # instead of printing an error.
    parent=$(git rev-parse -q --verify "$object^1" 2>/dev/null) || parent=""
    # -z because gofmt prints a path literally and git C-quotes any path it
    # cannot write that way (`"core/\303\244.go"`, quotes included), so without
    # it such a file would never match and would always count as the commit's
    # own. The records go through a file, not a pipe, so a failed diff is seen
    # (a pipeline's status is tr's). `tr '\0' '\n'` gives back the one case -z
    # exists for beyond quoting, a newline inside a name: such a file splits
    # into two lines that match nothing, and it too counts as the commit's own.
    # That errs toward blocking and a longer report, never toward a missed file,
    # which is what lets a shell with no NUL-safe read afford it.
    changed=""
    if [ -n "$parent" ]; then
        if git diff --name-only -z --no-renames "$parent" "$object" -- '*.go' >"$tmp_root/changed" 2>/dev/null; then
            changed=$(tr '\0' '\n' <"$tmp_root/changed")
        else
            parent=""
        fi
    fi
    parent_status=""
    if [ -n "$parent" ] && [ -f "$tmp_root/$parent.status" ]; then
        parent_status="$tmp_root/$parent.status"
    fi

    status="$tmp_root/$object.status"
    : >"$status"
    own=""
    before=""
    pushed_count=0
    noted_count=0
    while IFS= read -r f; do
        [ -n "$f" ] || continue
        if [ -z "$parent" ] || printf '%s\n' "$changed" | grep -Fxq -- "$f"; then
            own="$own    $f
"
            echo "pushed $f" >>"$status"
        elif [ -n "$parent_status" ] && grep -Fxq -- "pushed $f" "$parent_status"; then
            pushed_count=$((pushed_count + 1))
            echo "pushed $f" >>"$status"
        elif [ -n "$parent_status" ]; then
            noted_count=$((noted_count + 1))
            echo "before $f" >>"$status"
        else
            before="$before    $f
"
            echo "before $f" >>"$status"
        fi
    done <<FILES
$files
FILES

    blocks=no
    if [ -n "$own" ] || [ "$pushed_count" -gt 0 ] || [ "$mode" = "last" ]; then
        blocks=yes
    fi
    short=""
    [ -z "$parent" ] || short=$(git rev-parse --short "$parent" 2>/dev/null)

    if [ "$blocks" = "yes" ]; then
        echo "pre-push: $label is not gofmt-clean:" >&2
        failed=1
    else
        echo "pre-push: note: $label has unformatted files from before this push; only the tip is held to them:" >&2
    fi
    printf '%s' "$own" >&2
    if [ "$pushed_count" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "    + $pushed_count unchanged since $short, listed above" >&2
    fi
    if [ -n "$before" ]; then
        echo "    from before this push, unchanged by it:" >&2
        printf '%s' "$before" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
    fi
    if [ "$noted_count" -gt 0 ]; then
        echo "    + $noted_count from before this push, unchanged since $short, noted above" >&2
    fi
    return 0
}

# `read` splits on IFS whitespace. The four fields git sends never contain
# spaces (ref names cannot), so a plain read is enough.
while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
    [ -n "${local_sha:-}" ] || continue
    is_zero_sha "$local_sha" && continue

    if [ "$every" = "false" ]; then
        check_tree "$local_ref ($local_sha)" "$local_sha"
        continue
    fi

    # Every commit this push adds to the ref, oldest first. `cat-file -e`
    # asks whether the remote's old tip is here at all: after someone else's
    # push that this clone has not fetched, it is not, and a range from it
    # would be an error rather than a list.
    if ! is_zero_sha "$remote_sha" && git cat-file -e "$remote_sha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
        commits=$(git rev-list --reverse "$remote_sha..$local_sha" 2>/dev/null) || commits=""
    else
        commits=$(git rev-list --reverse "$local_sha" --not --remotes 2>/dev/null) || commits=""
    fi
    count=$(printf '%s\n' "$commits" | grep -c .)
    if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
        # Nothing new (a push of a ref to an older commit, or a tag object
        # rev-list could not walk): the tip is still what CI will check.
        check_tree "$local_ref ($local_sha)" "$local_sha"
        continue
    fi
    if [ "$count" -gt "$EVERY_COMMIT_MAX" ]; then
        echo "pre-push: $local_ref adds $count commits, more than $EVERY_COMMIT_MAX; checking its tip only." >&2
        check_tree "$local_ref ($local_sha)" "$local_sha"
        continue
    fi
    # rev-list --reverse ends at the ref's tip commit (the commit a tag
    # object points at, for a tag), which is the one CI's gofmt runs on.
    last=$(printf '%s\n' "$commits" | tail -n 1)
    for commit in $commits; do
        subject=$(git log -1 --format=%s "$commit" 2>/dev/null)
        mode=commit
        [ "$commit" = "$last" ] && mode=last
        check_tree "$local_ref at $commit ($subject)" "$commit" "$mode"
    done
done <<REFS
$refs
REFS

if [ "$failed" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'MSG'

CI's Go job fails at gofmt on this, and every step after it (vet, -race tests,
the WASM build, wasm/verify) is skipped. To fix:

    gofmt -w <files above>
    go run ./internal/apidoc/gen   # docs/api cites source lines; a reformat can move them
    git commit, then push again

To push anyway: git push --no-verify

A commit before the tip is fixed by rewriting it, e.g. `git rebase -i` and an
`edit` on that commit; or check the tip only, as CI does:
git -c grmob.prepush.everyCommit=false push
MSG
    exit 1
fi

exit 0
